WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.000
Symmetric encryption uses shared secret keys.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:10.340
The same key, or closely related secret material managed by the library or protocol, is used to protect and recover data.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:18.080
This makes symmetric encryption efficient, but it also means every party that can decrypt must be trusted to hold the relevant secret appropriately.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:30.300
Modern application designs often prefer authenticated encryption with associated data, commonly called AEAD, when the goal is to protect data confidentiality and detect unauthorized modification.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:36.040
The authentication part matters because ciphertext should not be treated as trustworthy simply because it is encrypted.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:41.220
The application needs to know whether protected data was altered or used in the wrong context.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.560
Nonce and initialization-value handling must follow the library rules exactly.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.940
Some modes require uniqueness.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.400
Some require unpredictable values.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.740
Some libraries generate or manage the value for the caller.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:04.880
The application should not substitute a convenient counter, timestamp, random-looking string, or reused value unless that pattern is explicitly supported and reviewed.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.540
Associated data can bind context to protected data without encrypting that context.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:20.280
For example, a record type, version, tenant identifier, or protocol state can be authenticated so the protected data is only accepted in the intended setting.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:25.460
This is useful, but only when the context is chosen deliberately and verified consistently.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.800
Encryption does not replace validation or authorization.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:40.540
After decryption and authentication succeed, the application still needs to parse the plaintext safely, validate fields, enforce permissions, and handle malformed or unexpected content.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:46.600
Cryptography can protect a channel or object, but application logic must still decide what the data means.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:58.820
Reviewers should look for clear key purpose, approved AEAD usage where appropriate, correct nonce handling, authenticated context, return-value checks, and safe behavior on verification failure.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.200
If any of those pieces are unclear, the encryption design is incomplete.
